# Command-level tests (PRD §6.13): exercise a Tauri command handler end to
# end — deserialise input, call into `aeroworship-core`, assert on the
# returned/emitted shape — as opposed to `tests/unit/`, which tests a pure
# function directly with no `tauri` runtime involved, and `tests/perf/`,
# which asserts on latency/memory rather than correctness.
#
# Nothing lives here yet because no FR has shipped a command. The first item
# under M1 (FR-1xx) that adds a `#[tauri::command]` owns the first file here.
#
# Likely shape, not yet decided by any item: a `tauri::test` mock app per
# PRD §6.3's command boundary ("deserialise, call core, emit or return"), so
# these tests catch a command that skips core and reimplements logic inline —
# the ADR-0008 violation unit tests on `aeroworship-core` alone cannot see,
# because they never invoke the command layer at all.
#
# What does NOT belong here:
#   - Parsing, splitting, serialisation, path resolution — correctness-critical
#     logic that PRD §6.1/§6.3 require to live in `aeroworship-core` as pure
#     functions, tested in `tests/unit/` (or the crate's own `tests/`, Cargo
#     convention) without a `tauri` runtime.
#   - Timing, memory or soak assertions (NFR-01…NFR-07) — `tests/perf/`.
#   - Real congregation content in any `fixtures/` subdirectory this suite
#     creates. `.gitignore` unignores `**/fixtures/**/*.aero` and
#     `*.aerotpl` specifically so NFR-15/NFR-28 fixtures can live in the repo,
#     and ADR-0023's staged-content guard (`scripts/check-fixture-content.js`,
#     wired into `npm test`) rejects any `.aero`/`.aerotpl`/media file staged
#     under a `fixtures/` directory that is not declared synthetic in a
#     `fixtures.manifest.json` sitting at that directory's root — see that
#     script's module doc before adding the first fixture here, in particular
#     for FR-705's relink bundles (`fixtures/relink-a/media/`,
#     `fixtures/relink-b/media/`), which are exactly the case the guard exists
#     to catch.
